=== every class class_or_fail accepts, what it needs, and whether it reaches a person
escalation    asking            human-required  notifies
escalation    question          human-required  notifies
escalation    ladder-end        human-required  notifies
escalation    unfinished-twice  replan          notifies
escalation    blocked           replan          notifies
escalation    merge-failed      human-required  notifies
escalation    other             human-required  notifies
escalation    disagreement      human-required  notifies
escalation    omitted           human-required  notifies
escalation    model_not_found   human-required  notifies
escalation    dispatch-failed   human-required  notifies
escalation    plan-unreadable   human-required  notifies
escalation    plan-unparseable  human-required  notifies
escalation    main-broken       human-required  notifies
escalation    baton-unhealthy   HOST-EXPLAINED  silent
notification  rate_limit        human-required  notifies
notification  billing_error     human-required  notifies
notification  unrecoverable     human-required  notifies
notification  transient         human-required  notifies
notification  stall             human-required  notifies
notification  long-running      human-required  notifies
notification  blocked_by        human-required  notifies
notification  distant_wait_for  human-required  notifies
notification  prompt-lost       human-required  notifies
notification  gap               human-required  notifies
notification  takeover-silent   human-required  notifies

=== and the other direction: no arm of disposition_of names a class class_or_fail does not.
=== A dead arm is invisible to the listing above, and it is how a class removed from the log
=== taxonomy would leave a disposition behind that nothing can ever ask for.
  the two lists are the same, in both directions

=== the one arm that reads the carries: a bounded choice the session enumerated
with options:    AI-resolvable
one option:      AI-resolvable
a lone string, which a session may still write where the contract says a list:
                 AI-resolvable
=== and the shapes a session writes when it had none, which must not look bounded
empty options:   human-required
empty string:    human-required
an object:       human-required
the number nil:  human-required
no options:      human-required
no carries:      human-required
carries not json:human-required

=== the second arm that reads the carries, and the only disposition that is not a constant.
=== The listing above asked about the class with no evidence at all and got human-required,
=== which is what a gap nothing accounts for really needs. What moves it is one field, written
=== by the parser in lib/host.sh and by nothing else.
host explained it:   HOST-EXPLAINED
=== and every other reading of the same field, each of which reaches a person
complete, no sleep:  human-required
not enough sleep:    human-required
the read failed:     human-required
a doubt inside:      human-required
=== and the shapes that must not be read as an explanation, because unknown is not asleep
no host object:      human-required
host is null:        human-required
assessed is empty:   human-required
assessed is a list:  human-required
no carries:          human-required
carries not json:    human-required
=== the answer this function wrote last time is not evidence, and cannot talk it into itself
disposition only:    human-required

=== a class neither list holds is refused, not defaulted
exit 1
exit 1

=== a disposition is not a class, and asking the wrong question fails where it is visible
exit 2
